Who is the most important person in the kingdom?
At that time, the disciples came to Jesus. They asked him, "Who is the most important person in the kingdom of heaven?"
Jesus called a little child to him. He stood him among them.
Jesus said, "Truly I tell you, unless you change and become like children, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven. Whoever humbles himself like this child is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven."
Whoever becomes like this child in their innocence and freedom from pride, is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
Whoever accepts a little one in my name accepts me.
But what if one of these little ones who believe in me were to cause one of these little ones who believe in me to sin? It would be better for him to have a great millstone hung around his neck and to be thrown into the depths of the sea.
What a terrible thing is about to befall the world because of the things that drive people into sin! Things like this must come to pass. But what a terrible thing it is for those who cause it!
If your hand or foot causes you to stumble, cut it off and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with your hand or foot cut off, than to be thrown into hell with your hands and feet intact. In hell, the fire never goes out.
If your eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of God with one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into hell, where the worms never die and the fire is never quenched. The story of the lost sheep.
Watch yourselves so that you do not look down on any of these little ones. I tell you this: their angels in heaven are able to go at any time to see my Father who is in heaven.
.
What do you think? Let's suppose a man has 100 sheep, and one of them goes astray. Won't he leave the 99 sheep on the hills? Won't he go and look for the one that went astray?
What I am about to tell you is true. If that sheep is found, he is happier with that one sheep than with the ninety-nine that did not go astray.
It is the same with your Father in heaven. He does not want any of these little ones to be lost. When someone wrongs you
If your brother wrongs you, go to him. Tell him what he did wrong. Keep the matter between you two. If he listens to you, you have won him back.
But what if he does not listen to you? Then take one or two others along with you. The Scriptures say: "Every word of God is confirmed. A double-minded man is unstable in all his ways." (Deuteronomy 19:15)
But what if he also refuses to listen to witnesses? Then tell the church. And what if he refuses to listen even to the church? Then do not treat him as a brother. Instead, treat him as you would treat a wicked person or a tax collector. **Explanation of Modern Standard Arabic (MSA) choices:** * **"ولكن ماذا لو رفض أيضا الاستماع إلى"** translates to **"But what if he also refuses to listen to"**
What I am about to tell you is true. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven. What you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
Again, this is what I tell you. Be aware, if two of you on earth agree about anything you ask for, it will be done for you by my Father who is in heaven.
"Wherever two or three are gathered in my name, I am there among them." The servant who did not have compassion.
Peter came to Jesus and asked him, "Lord, how many times must I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus answered, "Truly I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times.
The kingdom of heaven is like a king who wanted to settle accounts with all his servants regarding the debts owed to him.
And while the king was about to start that, a man was brought to him who owed him millions of dollars.
The man was not able to pay. Therefore, his master gave an order. The man, his wife, children, and everything he possessed were to be sold to pay what he owed.
The servant fell to his knees before him. "Grant me time," he pleaded. "I will restore everything."
The lady felt sorry for him. She forgave him for what he owed and let him go.
But then that valet came out and found one of the other valets to whom he owed a few dollars. So he grabbed him and began choking him. "Pay what you owe me!" he said.
The other servant fell to his knees. "Give me some time," he pleaded. "I will get you the money."
But the first servant refused. Instead, he went and ordered the man to be thrown into prison. The man would remain held there until he could repay what he owed.
The other servants witnessed what happened. It deeply disturbed them. So they went and told their master everything that had happened.
Then the master called the first servant. He said to him, "You wicked servant, I forgave you all that you owed me, because you requested it of me."
Haven't you been obligated to show mercy to the other servant, just as I have shown mercy to you?
His master became angry and handed him over to the executioners, who would discipline him until he repaid what he owed him.
This is how my Father in heaven will treat each of you unless you forgive your brother from your heart.
Jesus teaches about divorce.
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went into the region of Judea on the other side of the Jordan River.
A great multitude followed him, and he cured them there.
Some Pharisees came, trying to test him. They asked him, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for every cause?"
Jesus said, "Haven't you read that he who created them from the beginning 'made them male and female'?'" (Genesis 1:27)
He said, "Therefore a man shall leave his father and his mother, and shall cling to his wife, and they shall become one flesh." (Genesis 2:24)
"Lisa is not yet two, but one. So a man shall not leave the one he joined to another."
They asked, "Why then did Moses command that if a man wished to divorce his wife, he should give her a divorce document and send her away from his house?"
Jesus answered, "Moses allowed you to divorce your wives because your hearts were hard. But it was not like that from the beginning."
Here is what I say to you. Whoever divorces his wife and marries another woman commits adultery. And a man is not permitted to divorce his wife unless she has been unfaithful to him."
His disciples said to him, "If this is the case between a husband and wife, it is better not to marry."
Jesus answered, "Not everyone is able to accept the idea of remaining single. Only those who have been helped to live without marrying can accept it.
Some men are not able to have children because they were born that way. Some were made that way by other people. Others have made themselves that way in order to serve the Kingdom of Heaven. A person who is able to accept living that way, should do so." Little children are brought to Jesus.
Some people brought their little children to Jesus. They wanted him to place his hands on the children and pray for them. But the disciples told the people to stop.
Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these."
Jesus laid his hands on them. Then he went from there. Jesus and the Rich Young Man
A man approached Jesus and asked him, "Teacher, what good deed must I do to have eternal life?"
Why do you ask me about what is good?" Jesus answered. "No one is good—except God alone. If you want to enter life, keep the commandments."
"Which ones?" the man asked. Jesus said, " 'Do not murder, do not commit adultery, do not steal, do not give false testimony.' "
"Honor your father and your mother." (Exodus 20:12-16; Deuteronomy 5:16-20) and "You shall love your neighbor as yourself." (Leviticus 19:18)
The young man said, "I have obeyed all those commandments." "What else must I do?"
Jesus answered, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give to the poor. Then you will have treasure in heaven. And then come, follow me."
When the young man heard that, he departed, sad. And he was of great wealth.
Then Jesus said to his disciples: "Truly I say to you, that it is hard for a rich man to enter into the kingdom of the heavens.
I also tell you: It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle, than for a rich person to enter the kingdom of God."
When the disciples heard this, they were very astonished and asked, saying, "Who can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "This is impossible for mortals, but for God, all things are possible."
Peter answered him, "We have left everything to follow you! What then will be our reward?"
Jesus said to them, "What I am about to say to you is true. When all things are made new, the Son of Man will sit on his glorious throne. Then you who have followed me will also sit on twelve thrones. You will judge the twelve tribes of Israel."
Everyone who has left houses or family or fields for my sake will receive a hundred times more and will inherit eternal life.
But many who are first will be last, and many who are last will be first.
The Story of the Vineyard Workers
"The kingdom of the heavens is like a man who had a field. And he went out early before dawn to hire laborers for his vineyard."
He agreed to pay them the usual wage for a full day's work. Then he sent them into his vineyard.
At about nine o'clock in the morning, he went out again. He saw others standing in the market square doing nothing.
And he said to them, "You also go to my vineyard, and I will give you what is right."
So they left. "He went out again around noon and at three o'clock and did the same thing.
At about five o'clock he went out and found others still standing about. "Why do you stand about here all day long and do nothing?" he asked.
" 'Because no one has hired us,' they answered. "He said to them, 'You also go and work in my vineyard.' "
And when evening came, the landowner spoke to the manager over the workers and said to him, "Call the workers and give them their pay, beginning with the last ones and then the first ones."
The workers who were hired came around five o'clock. Each of them received the usual day's wages.
When the first workers came, they thought they would receive more. However, each of them also received the usual day's wage.
When they took possession, they began complaining about the owner.
And they said, "These people who were hired, only worked for one hour." "You paid them the same as us. We did most of the work and were exposed to the scorching sun all day."
One of the owners answered him: "Friend, I am dealing fairly with you. Did you not agree to work for the usual day wage?
Take your money and leave. I want to give those I recently hired the same salary I gave you.
Do I not have the right to do as I please with my own money? Do you feel deceived because I gave generously to others?
Therefore, those who are last will be first, and those who are first will be last." Jesus speaks again about his coming death.
Jesus was going up to Jerusalem. On the way, he took the twelve disciples aside to speak to them.
He said, "We are going up to Jerusalem." "The Son of Man will be betrayed to the chief priests and the teachers of the law. They will condemn him to death."
Then they will hand him over to people who are not Jews. These people will mock him, flog him, and then crucify him on a cross. On the third day, he will rise from the dead!" A woman asks Jesus for a favor
The mother of my son Zebedee came to Jesus with her sons. She knelt down and asked him for a request.
Jesus asked, "What do you want?" And she said, "Promise me that one of my two sons will sit at your right hand in your kingdom. Promise me that the other will sit at your left hand."
Jesus said to them, "You do not know what you are asking for. Can you drink the cup of suffering that I am to drink?" They said, "We can."
Jesus said to them, "Truly, you will drink from my cup. And I have no right to say who will sit at my right or left hand. These places have been prepared for those whom my Father has prepared."
The other ten pupils heard about it; they were angry with the two brothers.
Jesus called them together and said, "You know that the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and their high officials exercise authority over them.
Do not be like this. Instead, anyone who wants to be important among you must be your servant.
And whoever desires to be first among you, let him be your servant.
Behold the Son of Man. For he did not come to be served, but to serve and to give his life as a ransom for many." Blind eyes now see.
Jesus and His disciples were leaving Jericho. A great throng was following Him.
Two blind men were sitting by the roadside. When they heard that Jesus was passing by, they called out, "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on us!"
The crowd bade them be silent. "Hush!" they said to the two men. But the men cried out all the more, "Lord, son of David, have mercy on us!"
Jesus stopped and called them, saying, "What do you want Me to do for you?" He asked them.
"Lord," they answered, "we want to be given the ability to see."
Jesus felt deep concern for them. He touched their eyes. Immediately they were able to see. And they followed him.
Jesus' entry into Jerusalem
And when they drew near to Jerusalem to Bethphage and Bethany, at the Mount of Olives, Jesus sent two of his disciples.
He said to them, "Go to the village before you. As soon as you get there, you will find a donkey tied up, and its foal with it. Untie them and bring them both to me."
If anyone tells you anything, say, 'The Lord needs them.' And the owner will send them out immediately."
This happened so that what was spoken by the prophet's tongue would be fulfilled. It is written.
Say to the daughter of Zion, 'Behold, your king is coming to you, gentle and riding on a donkey, and on a colt, the foal of a donkey.' 'Rejoice greatly, O daughter of Zion! Shout, O daughter of Jerusalem! Behold, your king is coming to you, righteous and having salvation, gentle and riding on a donkey, and on a colt, the foal of a donkey.' (Zechariah 9:9)
The disciples went and did what Jesus had commanded them.
They brought the donkey and the colt, and they laid their clothes on them, and Jesus sat on the clothes.
The great multitude stood, and they cast their garments in the way; and others cut down branches from the trees, and cast them in the way.
Some of them went ahead of him, and others followed. They all cried out: "Hosanna to the Son of David!" "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" (Psalm 118:26) "Hosanna in the highest!"
And when Jesus entered Jerusalem, the whole city was stirred up. And the people asked, "Who is this?"
And the multitude answered, "This is Jesus, the prophet, from Nazareth of Galilee." Jesus Cleanses the Temple
Jesus entered the temple court. And he began to drive out all those who were selling and buying there. And he overturned the tables of the money-changers. And he overturned the benches of those who sold doves.
He said to them, "It is written, 'The Lord has said, "My house shall be called a house of prayer for all the nations." (Isaiah 56: 7) You, however, are making it 'a den of robbers.'" (Jeremiah 7: 11)
And he came to Jesus in the temple, a blind man and those with disabilities; and he healed them there.
The chief priests and the teachers of the law saw the wonders that he did and the children in the temple crying out "Hosanna to the Son of David!" They were indignant and asked him, "Do you hear what they are
"Do you hear what these children are saying?" they asked him. Jesus answered, "Yes, have you not read in the scriptures: 'From the lips of children and infants you have ordained praise'? (Psalm 8:2)
Then Jesus left the crowds, went out of the city to Bethany, and spent the night there. The Withering of the Fig Tree
Early in the morning, Jesus was on his way back to Jerusalem. He was hungry.
He saw a fig tree by the roadside. He went up to it but found nothing on it but leaves. Then he said to it, "May you never bear fruit!" Immediately the fig tree withered.
When the disciples saw it, they marveled and said, "How did the fig tree wither immediately?"
Jesus said, "Truly I tell you, if you have faith in God, and do not doubt, you will do what was done to the fig tree; and you will say to this mountain, 'Be removed from here to there,' and it will be done. Nothing will be impossible for you."
"If you believe, you will receive whatever you ask for when you pray." Jesus' authority is being questioned.
Jesus entered the temple court. As he taught there, the chief priests and the elders of the people came to him. "By what authority are you doing these things?" they asked. "Who gave you this authority?"
Jesus said, "I also will ask you one question. If you tell me, then I also will tell you by what authority I do these things."
From where did John's baptism come? Was it from heaven, or from men?" And they reasoned with one another, saying, "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will say, 'Why then did you not believe him?'
But what if we say: "Of the men"? We fear people. Everyone believes that John was a prophet."
They said to Jesus, "We do not know." Jesus said, "Then I will not tell you by what authority I do these things. The Parable of the Two Sons
What do you think about this? A man had two sons. He went to the first son and said, "My son, go and work in the vineyard today."
The son said, 'I will not.' But he changed his mind later and went.
Then the father went to the other son. He told him the same thing. And the son answered, "I will, sir." But he did not go.
Which of the two sons did the will of his father?" "The first," they answered. Jesus said to them, "Truly I tell you: tax collectors and prostitutes enter the kingdom of God ahead of you.
John came to show you the right way to live. And you didn't believe him. But tax collectors and prostitutes believed him. You have seen this. But even then you didn't turn away from your sins and believe him. The story of the tenants.
Here is another story. A man owned some land and planted a vineyard. He put a wall around it. He dug a pit for a winepress in it. He also built a watchtower. He rented the vineyard to some farmers. Then he went on a trip.
And when the harvest time approached, he sent his servants to the farmers and said to them: Gather my fruits of grapes, figs, and olives.
But the tenants seized his servants, and they beat one of them and killed another, and they threw the third one down with stones in order to kill him.
Then the man sent other servants to the farmers, and he sent more than he had sent at first. And the farmers dealt with them in the same way.
And finally, he sent them his son. And he said, "My son will be respected."
But the tenants saw the son coming. And they said to each other, "This is the one who will get all the owner's property someday. Come, let's kill him. And then everything will be ours."
And they seized him, brought him out of the vineyard, and killed him.
When the owner of the vineyard returns, what will he do to those tenants?
"The evil ones will perish," they answered. "Then the vineyard will be rented to other tenants. They will give him his share of the crop at harvest time."
Jesus said to them, "Have you not read in the scriptures what is written, 'The stone which the builders rejected has become the head of the corner. This was from the Lord, and it is amazing in our eyes'? (Psalm 118: 22, 23)"
So this is what I say to you. The kingdom of God will be taken from you. It will be given to people who will bear fruit with it.
And everyone who falls on that stone will be shattered by it. But the stone that strikes anything will destroy it."
The chief priests and the Pharisees heard about Jesus's stories. They knew he was talking about them.
So they sought how they might hold him; but they feared the people. For the people were already convinced that Jesus was a prophet.
The Story of the Wedding Feast
Jesus told them more stories. He said:
The kingdom of heaven is like a certain king who prepared a wedding feast for his son.
He sent his slaves to those who had been invited to the feast, and the slaves said to them, "Come," but they would not come.
Then he sent other servants, saying to them, 'Tell those who were invited, "I have prepared my dinner, and I have killed my fattened oxen. All is ready; come to the feast."'
But people did not pay attention to that. So, one of them went to his field. And another went to his work.
The rest seized his servants, abused them, and killed them.
The king was filled with rage. He sent his army to destroy them. They killed those murderers and burned their city.
Then the king said to his servants, "The wedding feast is ready. But the guests I invited were not worthy to come."
Go to the crossroads. Invite to dinner anyone you can find.
The servants went out into the streets and gathered everyone they found, good and bad. Soon the wedding hall was filled with guests.
The king entered to see the guests. He noticed a man there who was not wearing wedding clothes.
My friend, he asked: How did you get in here without your wedding clothes? The man could not think of anything to say.
Then the king said to his servants, "Tie his hands and feet. Throw him outside in the darkness. There people will cry and grind their teeth."
Many are called, but few are chosen. As for paying taxes to Caesar, the original text does not contain a direct question about this topic. However, it can be inferred that the question concerns the legitimacy or morality of paying taxes to the governing authority, represented by "Caesar" in this historical context.
And the Pharisees went out, and took counsel together against Jesus, how they might entangle him in his talk.
They sent their disciples to Him, and they sent the Herodians with them. And they said, "Teacher, we know that you are a truthful person. You teach the way of God in truth. And you do not care about people's opinions or their commands. And you do not pay attention to their status or their authority." (Note: The original text contained URLs and references to specific Bible verses and commentaries, which I have preserved as untranslatable parts of the input.)
"Then tell me, what is your opinion? Is it right to pay the tribute to Caesar or not?"
But Jesus knew their evil plans. He said, "Hypocrites! Why are you trying to trap me?"
"Show me the coin that people use to pay the tax." So they brought a silver coin.
He asked them, "Whose picture is this? And whose words are these?"
"Caesar," they said. Then he said to them, "Give to Caesar what is Caesar's. And give to God what is God's."
When they heard, they were astonished. So they left him and went away. Marriage at the resurrection.
That day, the Sadducees came to Jesus with a question. They do not believe that people rise from the dead.
"Master," they said, "this is what Moses told us. If a man dies without having children, his brother must marry the widow. He must have children to carry on his brother's name."
There were seven brothers among us. The first one got married and died, and he had no children, so he left his wife for his brother.
The same thing happened to the second and third brothers. It happened right up to the seventh brother.
Finally, the woman died.
Now, when the dead rise, who will be his wife? All seven of them were married to her."
Jesus answered, "You are mistaken, because you do not know the Scriptures, nor the power of God.
When the dead are resurrected, they will not marry. Nor will their fathers give them away in marriage. They will be like the angels in heaven.
What about the raising of the dead? Have you not read what God has told you?
He said, "I am the God of Abraham. I am the God of Isaac. and I am the God of Jacob." (Exodus 3:6) He is not the God of the dead. He is the God of the living."
When the crowds heard this, they were amazed at his teaching. The Great Commandment.
The Pharisees heard that the Sadducees had not been able to answer Jesus. So the Pharisees gathered together.
One of them was an expert in the law, and he began to test Jesus by asking him questions.
"Master," he asked, "which is the greatest commandment in the law?"
Jesus answered, " 'Love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your mind.' (Deuteronomy 6: 5)
This is the first and most important commandment.
And the second is like it: 'You shall love your neighbor as yourself.' (Leviticus 19:18)
All that is written in the law and the prophets depends on these two commandments." "Who is the son of Christ?"
And the Pharisees gathered together, and Jesus asked them,
What do you think about the Messiah? Whose son is he? "The son of David," they answered.
He said to them, "Then why does David call him 'Lord'? For the Holy Spirit spoke through David himself. David said,
The Lord said to my Lord, "Sit at my right hand, until I make your enemies your footstool." (Psalm 110:1)
If David called him "my Lord," how could he be his son?"
No one could answer him with a single word. From that day on, no one dared to ask him any more questions.
Jesus's Judgment of the Pharisees and Teachers of the Law
Jesus spoke to the crowds and to his disciples.
He said, "The scribes and Pharisees sit on the seat of Moses."
Therefore, you must obey them. Do everything they tell you to do. But do not do what they do. They do not practice what they preach.
They bind up heavy loads and place them on the shoulders of others. But they themselves are not willing to lift a finger to move them.
All they do they do so that others may see. On their foreheads and their arms they wear small boxes containing texts from the Holy Book. They make the boxes very wide. And they make the tassels on their coats very long.
They love to sit in the place of honor at banquets. They also love to have the most important seats in temples.
They like to be greeted in the markets. They like it when people call them "Rabbi".
But you should not call me "Rabbi". You have only one teacher, and you are all brothers.
Do not call anyone on earth 'father.' You have one Father, and he is in heaven.
You should not be called "Teacher." You have one teacher, the Christ.
The most important person among you will be your servant.
He who rises falls, and he who falls rises.
How terrible the punishment that will be suffered by the scribes and Pharisees who are hypocrites! You hypocrites! You lock up the kingdom of heaven in the faces of people, you do not enter, nor do you let those who are entering enter.
.
How severe is it for you, you writers and interpreters who are puffed up with pride! You who are like tombs that are defiled! You walk long distances in order to convince one person of your religion, then you make him defiled and polluted with weakness like you!
How great is your affliction, you who are blind guides! You say: "If anyone swears by the name of the temple, it has no meaning. But if anyone swears by the name of the gold of the temple, he must keep his oath."
You are blind and foolish! Which is more important? Is it the gold? Or is it the Temple which makes the gold sacred?
You also say: "If anyone vows by the name of the altar, there is nothing to it. But if anyone vows a gift to be placed on it, he must fulfill his vow."
O ye blind! Which is more important? Is it the gift? Or is it the altar that makes the gift sacred?
Therefore, anyone who swears by the altar swears by the altar and by everything on it.
And every one that swears by the altar, his oath is by the altar and by him that dwelleth therein.
And whoever swears by the name of the sky, he has sworn by the name of the throne of God, and by the name of Him who sits upon it.
Woe to you, teachers of the law and Pharisees, you hypocrites! You tithe mint, dill and cumin, yet you neglect the more important matters of the law: justice, mercy and faithfulness. You should have practiced the latter, without neglecting the former.
You blind guides! You strain out a gnat, and swallow a camel.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! You clean the outside of the cup and dish, but inside they are full of greed. You want to be praised by men, but you do not get the commendation of God.
You blind Pharisee! First clean the inside of the cup and of the platter; then what is outside of them shall be clean also.
How profound is your sorrow, you teachers of mankind, you Pharisees! You falsifiers! For you are like painted tombs, beautiful on the outside. But inside, they are full of dead men's bones. And they are also full of other unclean and impure things.
It's the same with you. From the outside, you start to look like you're doing the right thing. But on the inside, you are full of wrong. You pretend to be what you are not.
Oh, what a fearful sight! You teachers of the law and you Pharisees hypocrites! You build tombs for the prophets and you decorate the tombs of the righteous
And you would have said, 'If only we had been sent down during the days of those who passed away before us, we would not have done what they did.' And we would not have helped them to kill the prophets.
So you will bear witness against yourselves that you are the sons of those who murdered the prophets.
Complete the transgressions from those who were before you!
O nest of poisonous vipers! How will you escape the sentence of hellfire?
Therefore, I am sending prophets, wise men, and teachers to you. You will kill some of them, you will crucify some of them on a cross, and you will scourge some of them within your temples. You will expel them from one city to another.
So you will pay the price for the blood of all the righteous that has been shed on earth. I mean, from the blood of righteous Abel to the blood of Zechariah, the son of Berekiah. Zechariah is the one you killed between the temple and the altar.
What I am about to tell you is true. All of this will happen to those who are alive now.
Oh Jerusalem! Oh Jerusalem! You are killing the prophets, and stoning those who are sent to you. How often I wanted to gather your children together, as a hen gathers her brood under her wings! But you would not let me!
Look, your house is left empty.
I tell you: You will not see me again until you say, 'Blessed is he who stands in the name of the Lord.'" (Psalm 118:26)
End Times Signs
Jesus left the temple. As he was going away, his disciples approached him. They wanted to draw his attention to the temple buildings.
"Do you see all these things?" Jesus asked. "Truly I tell you, not one stone will be left here upon another; all will be thrown down."
Jesus was sitting on the Mount of Olives. There, the disciples came to him privately. They said, "Tell us, when will this happen? And what will be the sign of your coming? What will be the sign of the end?"
Jesus answered, "Watch out! Be on guard against deceiving yourselves.
Many will come in my name, saying, 'I am the Messiah!' and they will deceive many.
You will hear of wars; you will even hear people talking about wars to come. Do not panic. Such things must happen. But the end is not yet. You will hear of wars; you will even hear people talking about wars to come. Do not panic. Such things must happen. But the end is not yet.
Nation will rise against nation, and kingdom against kingdom. There will be great earthquakes, famines and pestilences in various places.
All of these are the beginnings of labor.
Then they will hand you over to be tortured and killed. All the peoples will hate you for my sake."
At that time, many will fall away from the faith and will hate one another and deliver one another over to their enemies.
Many false prophets will appear. They will deceive many people.
Because lawlessness will increase, the love of many will grow cold.
But whoever endures to the end, that one will be saved.
You will be gladdened by these joyful tidings concerning the Kingdom in the world at large. And she will be a witness for all the nations. Then the end will come.
The prophet Daniel spoke of "the abomination that makes desolate." (Daniel 9:27; 11:31; 12:11) One day you will see standing in the holy place, the one who causes desolation. Let the reader understand this.
Then those who are in the region of Judea must flee to the mountains.
Nobody is getting off the roof to go to their house to get anything.
No one in the field should go back to get their coat.
Woe to the women who are pregnant in those days. Woe to the nursing mothers.
Pray that you do not have to flee in winter or on a Saturday.
There will be terrible suffering in those days. It will be worse than anything else that has happened in the world from the beginning of time until now. And there will never be anything like it again.
If the end of the age was not sealed, no one would be saved. But for the sake of God's chosen people, the end of the age will be sealed.
At that time, someone might tell you, "Look! Here is the Christ!" or, "Behold, here he is!" Do not believe it.
False messiahs and false prophets will appear, performing great signs and wonders, and thereby misleading, if possible, even the elect.
You see, I told you beforehand.
So, if someone tells you, "It's very far in the desert," don't go out there. Or if someone tells you, "It's deep in the house," don't believe it.
The lightning that comes from the east can be seen in the west; and so it will be when the Son of Man comes.
Vultures will gather wherever there is a carcass.
Immediately after the terrible suffering of those days, 'the sun will be darkened, the moon will not give its light, the stars will fall from the sky, and the heavens will be shaken.' (Isaiah 13:10; 34:4)
Then the sign of the Son of Man will appear in heaven, and all the nations of the earth will mourn. They will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven with power and great glory.
He will send his angels with a loud trumpet. They will gather his chosen people from all four directions. They will bring them from one end of the heavens to the other.
Look for the fig's fruit. When its branches are heavy and its leaves have sprouted, then you will know that summer is near.
In the same way, when you see all these things happening, you know that the end is near. It is right at the door.
What I am about to tell you is true. The people living at that time will certainly not die until all those things happen.
The heaven and the earth shall pass away: but my words shall not pass away. No man knows the day or the hour.
No one knows about that day or hour, not even the angels in heaven, nor the Son, but only the Father knows.
Remember how it was in the days of Noah. It will be the same when the Son of Man comes.
In the days before the flood, people were eating and drinking. They were marrying. They were giving their daughters in marriage. They did all these things until the day Noah entered the ark.
They did not know anything about what would happen until the flood came and took them all. That is how it will be when the Son of Man comes.
Two men will be in the field; one will be taken and the other left.
Two women are grinding with a manual hand mill. One is taken and the other is left.
So keep vigil for the Day when the sky will bring forth smoke clearly visible.
You must understand something. Let's suppose that the homeowner knew at what time of night the thief was coming. Then he would have stayed vigilant. He would not have allowed his house to be broken into.
Be ye also ready: for in an hour that ye think not, the Son of Man cometh.
Let us suppose a master entrusts one of his servants to oversee the rest of the servants in his house. The servant's task is to provide them with food at the appropriate time. The master wants a faithful and wise servant for this purpose.
It would be good for the servant if the master found him doing his work when the master returned.
What I am about to tell you is true. The master will put that servant in charge of all his possessions.
But let us suppose that the servant is wicked. Let us suppose that he says to himself: "My master has been absent for a long time."
Let's suppose he began by striking the other servants. And let's suppose he eats and drinks with those who indulge in drinking.
And the master of that servant will come on a day that the servant does not expect, and he will return at an hour that the servant does not know.
Then he cuts him to pieces, limb from limb, and sends him to where the sycophants go. And there the people insist on wailing, and they gnaw their teeth.
The Tale of Ten Maids of Honour
Here is what the kingdom of heaven will be like at that time. I took ten virgins, their lamps, and went out to meet the bridegroom.
Five of them were stupid. Five were wise.
The fools took their lamps, but they didn't take olive oil with them.
The wise ones took oil in jars with their lamps.
The groom hasn't come for a long time. Therefore, the bridesmaids all got tired and fell asleep.
At midnight someone shouted, "Here is the bridegroom! Come out to meet him!"
Then the bride's maidens all woke up and prepared their lamps.
The ignorant said to the wise: Give us some of your oil, for our lamps are about to go out.
"No," they answered. "It may not be enough for all of us. Instead, go to those who are selling the oil. Buy some for yourselves."
So they went to buy oil. And while they were going, the bridegroom arrived. So she went in with him, the bridesmaids prepared and ready for the wedding feast. Then the door was locked.
Later, the other bridesmaids came too. "Master! Master!" they said. "Open the door for us!"
He answered, "What I am about to tell you is true. I do not know you."
Therefore, be ready, because you do not know either the day or the hour when the bridegroom is coming. The Parable of the Three Servants.
And again, this is what the Kingdom of Heaven will be like. There was a man going on a journey. He sent for his slaves and entrusted them with his possessions.
He gave $10,000 to one. And he gave $4,000 to another. And he gave $2,000 to the third. The man gave each servant the amount of money that he knew the servant could take care of. Then he went on his trip.
The server that received ten thousand dollars immediately went and invested his money. He earned another ten thousand dollars.
Whoever got 4000 dollars, earned an additional 4000 dollars.
As for the man who received 2000 dollars, he went and dug a hole in the ground. He hid his master's money there.
After a long time, the master of those servants returned. He wanted to gather all the money they had made.
The man who had received $10,000 brought the other $10,000. He said, "Sir, you trusted me with $10,000. Look, I have earned another $10,000."
His master answered, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful over a little, I will set you over much. Enter into the joy of your master!"
The man who was carrying 4000 dollars also came. He said: 'Master, you trusted me and gave me 4000 dollars. Look, I have earned another 4000 dollars.'
His master answered, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful over a little, I will set you over much. Enter into the joy of your master!"
Then the man to whom he had paid two thousand dollars came. "Ah, sir," he said, "I knew you were a hard man. You reap where you have not sown. You gather crops where you have not scattered the seeds."
I was afraid. I went out and buried your two thousand dollars in the ground. You see, this is what concerns you.
Then his master answered him, 'You wicked and lazy servant! Did you know that you were supposed to put my money in the bank so that I could earn interest at the end of the year?
Well then, you should have put my money in the bank. When I came back, I would have taken it back with interest.
Then his master ordered the other servants: "Take 2000 dollars from him. And give it to the one who has 20000 dollars."
And every person who has, will be given more. He will have more than enough. But for any person who does not have, even what he has will be taken from him.
Cast out that contemptible lackey into the outer darkness, where the people will wail and grind their teeth.' The sheep and the goats.
The Son of Man will come in his glory, and all the angels will come with him. Then he will sit on his throne, and all the nations wil
All nations will be gathered before him. Then he will separate people one from another, as a shepherd separates the sheep from the goats.
He will place the sheep on his right and the goats on his left.
Then the king will speak to those on his right, saying, 'Blessed are those my Father has given me. Come, inherit the kingdom prepared for you since the foundation of the world.'
I was hungry. And you gave me something to eat. I was thirsty. And you gave me something to drink. I was a stranger. And you invited me in.
I needed clothes. And you provided them for me. I was sick. And you took care of me. I was in prison. And you came to visit me.
Then the righteous will answer, saying, 'Lord, when did we see you hungry and feed you? Or when did we see you thirsty and give you a drink?'
When did we see you as a stranger and invite you in? When did we see you in need of clothing and give it to you?
When did we see you sick or in prison and visit you?
The King will answer: "What I am about to tell you is the truth. Whatever you did to one of the least of these brothers, you did to me."
Then He will say to those on His left, 'Depart from Me, you cursed, into the eternal fire prepared for the devil and his angels.'
I was hungry. But you did not give me anything to eat. I was thirsty. But you did not give me anything to drink.
I was a stranger. But you did not invite me in. I needed clothes. But you did not give me any. I was sick and in prison. But you did not take care of me.
They will also answer: 'Lord, when did we see you hungry or thirsty and did not help you? When did we see you as a stranger or in need of clothes or sick or in prison and did not help you?'
He will answer: "What I am about to tell you is true. Whatever you did not do for one of the least of these, you did not do for me."
Then they go to be punished forever. As for those who did what is right, they will receive eternal life.
The plan to kill Jesus.
Jesus spoke all these words. Then he said to his disciples,
As you know, Easter is in two days. The Son of Man will be delivered to be crucified on a cross.
Then the chief priests and the elders of the people assembled. And they took counsel together in Caiaphas' palace, who was the high priest that year.
They had made plans to capture Jesus by cunning. They wanted to kill him.
"But not during the feast," they said. "Lest the people become stirred up." A woman poured perfume on Jesus.
Jesus was in Bethany. He was in the house of a man named Simon, who had a skin disease.
A woman came to Jesus with a sealed alabaster jar filled with very precious ointment. The woman poured the ointment on Jesus' head as He sat at the table.
And when the disciples saw it, they were indignant and said, "Why this waste?"
The perfume could have been sold for a high price. The money could have been given to the poor.
Jesus knowing this, said to them: "Why do you trouble this woman? For she has done me a good work."
You will always have the poor with you, but you will not always have me.
I poured perfume on my body in preparation for my burial.
What I am about to tell you is true. What I have done will be told wherever the good news about this gospel is preached to the whole world. It will be told for her memory." Judas agrees to hand over Jesus.
One of the twelve went to the chief priests, whose name was Judas Iscariot.
He asked, "What will you give me if I hand Jesus over to you?" So they paid him thirty pieces of silver.
From that time on, Judas watched for an opportunity to hand Jesus over to them. The Last Supper.
This was the first day of the Feast of Unleavened Bread. So the disciples came to Jesus, asking, "Where do you want us to prepare for you to eat the Passover meal?"
He answered, "Go into the city to so-and-so the man, and tell him, 'The Teacher says: The hour has come for me. I will keep the Passover at your house with my disciples.'"
So the disciples did what Jesus had instructed them to do. Prepare the Passover dinner.
And in the evening, Jesus was reclining at the table with the twelve.
And they were eating, he said: "Indeed, it is a right that is upon me. Indeed, he will hand me over to one of you to my enemy."
And the disciples were very sad. One after the other, they began to say to Him, "Not I, Lord, is it?"
Jesus answered, "He is the one who has dipped his hand with me in the bowl.
"Indeed, the son of man will go away just as it is written about him, but woe to the one who betrays the son of man! It would be better for him if he had not been born."
It was Judas who would betray him. He said, "Surely not I, Rabbi, am I?" Jesus answered, "Yes. You are the one."
While they were eating, Jesus took bread. He gave thanks, broke the bread, and gave it to his disciples. "Take this," he said, "and eat it. This is my body."
Then he took the cup, gave thanks, and gave it to them. And he said, "All of you drink from it.
This is my blood of the new covenant. It is poured out for the forgiveness of the sins of many.
Here is what I say to you. From now on, I will not drink wine with you again until the day I drink it with you in my Father's kingdom."
Then they sang "Hallelujah" and went out to the Mount of Olives. And Jesus said, "Truly I tell you, this very night, before the rooster crows twice, Peter will deny me three times."
Jesus said to them, "This very night, before it is over, you will all fall away because of me. It is written: 'The Lord said to my Lord, "Sit at my right hand, until I put your enemies under your feet."' (Psalm 110:1)
But after I am raised from the dead, I will go ahead of you into Galilee."
Peter answered: "Others might leave because of you. But I will never do so."
Jesus answered, "The statement I am about to make to you is accurate. This very night, before the cock crows, you will have denied knowing me three times."
But Peter said, "I must die with you, for I would never disown you!" And all the other disciples said the same thing. Jesus Prays in the Garden of Gethsemani
Then Jesus went with his disciples to a place called Gethsemani, and he said to them, "Sit here while I go and pray over there."
He took with him Peter and the sons of Zebedee, and he began to be sorrowful and troubled.
Then he said to them, "My soul is very sorrowful. I feel that I am very close to death. Stay here. Watch with me."
He went a little further. Then he fell on his face to the ground. And he prayed, saying, "Father, if it be possible, let this cup pass from me. Nevertheless, not as I will, but as you will."
Then he came back to his disciples and found them sleeping. He said to Peter, "Are you still unable to watch with me for one hour?"
Watch and pray. Then you will not fall into temptation when you are tempted. The spirit is willing, but the flesh is weak.
And Jesus went a second time and prayed, saying, "Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me; yet not what I will, but what you will."
Then he came back. He found them sleeping again. They couldn't keep their eyes open.
And he left them and went away also. And immediately he prayed also, saying:
Then he returned to the disciples and said to them, "Are you still sleeping and taking your rest? Look! The hour is already near, and the Son of Man will be betrayed into the hands of sinners."
"Go then! Behold, he is betraying me!" And the soldiers seized Jesus.
While Jesus was still speaking, Judas, one of the twelve, arrived with a large crowd armed with swords and clubs. The chief priests and elders had sent them.
Judas, who was to betray Jesus, arranged a signal with them. "The one I kiss is the man," he said. "Seize him."
So Judas immediately went to Jesus and said, "Greetings, Teacher!" And he kissed him.
Jesus answered, "Friend, do as you have come for." Then the men stepped forward. They seized Jesus and arrested him.
At that moment, one of Jesus' disciples drew his sword. He struck the high priest's servant, cutting off his ear.
Jesus said to him, "Put your sword back into its place, for all those who take the sword will perish by the sword."
Do you think I can't ask my father for help? He will send an army of more than 70,000 angels immediately.
But how then will the prophecies be fulfilled? They say it must happen this way."
At that time, Jesus addressed the crowds, saying, "Am I leading a band of armed men against you?" he asked them. "Should you not have come out against me with swords and sticks? Every day I was in the temple courts teaching. And you did not arrest me."
But in order that the words of the prophets might be fulfilled." Then all the disciples forsook him and fled. Jesus is taken to the Sanhedrin
Those who seized Jesus led him to Caiaphas the high priest. The scribes and elders had also gathered there.
He wasn't too far away, Peter followed Jesus. He went straight to the courtyard of the chief priest. He entered and sat down with the guards to see what would happen.
And the chief priests and all the council were seeking false testimony against Jesus to put him to death.
But they found no evidence, although many false witnesses came forward. Finally, two other witnesses came forward.
They said, "This man was saying, 'I am able to destroy the temple of God and in three days rebuild it.'"
Then the high priest stood up and questioned Jesus, saying, "Do you not hear the testimony that these men are bringing against you?
But Jesus answered nothing. Then the high priest said to him, "I adjure you by the living God, that you tell us, if you are the Christ, the Son of God."
"Yes. It is just as you say," Jesus answered. "But here is what I tell all of you. In the days ahead, you will see the Son of Man seated at the right hand of the Mighty One. And you will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven."
Then the high priest tore his clothes, and said, "He has blasphemed against God! Why do we still need witnesses? You have heard him say this blasphemy.
"What do you think?" "He must die!" they answered.
Then they spat in his face. They hit him with their fists. Others slapped him.
They said, "Prophecy to us, O Messiah! Who is it that struck you?" Peter says that he does not know Jesus.
Peter was sitting in the courtyard when a servant girl approached him saying, "You also were with Jesus the Galilean."
But in front of them all, Peter said that he was not. "I don't know what you are talking about," he said.
Then he went out to the gate leading to the courtyard. And there another woman saw him. And she said to the people, "This man was with Jesus of Nazareth."
He said again that he didn't know him. He swore, saying, "I don't know this man!"
And after a little while, those who stood by came to Peter and said to him, "Certainly you are one of them, for your way of speaking shows you."
Then Peter began to curse himself. He swore to them, saying, "I do not know this man!" At that, a rooster crowed.
Then Peter remembered what Jesus had said. "A cock will not crow," Jesus had told him. "before that, you will have said three times that you do not know me." Peter went out to the outer court. He collapsed and wept.
Judas hangs himself.
It was early in the morning. All the chief priests and the elders of the people decided to kill Jesus.
They bound him and led him away. Then they handed him over to Pilate, the governor.
Judas, who had betrayed him, saw that Jesus was condemned to death. He felt deep remorse and sorrow for what he had done. Therefore, he returned the thirty pieces of silver to the chief priests and elders.
He said: "I have sinned, for I handed over an innocent man." They answered him: "What is that to us? That is your problem."
Then Judas cast down the silver in the temple and departed, and went and hanged himself.
The high priests picked up the coins. "It is against the law," they said, "for us to put this money into the temple treasury. It is blood money. It has paid for the death of a man."
Therefore, they decided to use the money to buy a potter's field. People from other countries will be buried there.
This is why it was called "Blood Square" until this day.
Then the word of Jeremiah the prophet came, who had said, "They have taken the thirty pieces of silver, the price at which the people of Israel valued me."
And they took these wages and bought the potter's field with them, as the Lord commanded me." (Zechariah 11:12,13; Jeremiah 19:1-13; 32:6-9) Bringing Jesus before Pilate
Jesus stood before the governor. And the governor asked him, "Are you the King of the Jews?" Jesus answered, "Yes. It is as you say."
But when the chief priests and elders brought to Him false witnesses who testified against Him, He answered nothing.
Then Pilate asked him, "Don't you hear the testimony they are giving against you?"
But Jesus answered none of the accusations. And the governor was really astonished.
It was the custom of the governor on Easter to release one prisoner. And the people chose the prisoner whom they wanted to be released.
At that time they had a notorious prisoner named Barabbas.
When the crowd was gathered, Pilate asked them, "Which one do I release to you? Barabbas? Or Jesus who is called the Messiah?"
Pilate knew that the leaders were jealous. And he knew that this was the reason they had handed Jesus over to him.
While Pilate was sitting in the judgment hall, his wife sent him a message saying, "Have nothing to do with this righteous man; for he has done many signs. Before they speak, I will warn you what to do. For as you are, you are not able to support a wife."
But the priests and elders of the people persuaded the crowds to ask for Barabbas to be released and Jesus to be killed.
"Which one do you want me to release for you?" asked the governor. "Barabbas," they answered.
"Then what shall I do with Jesus called Christ?" Pilate asked. And they all answered him, "Crucify him!"
"Why? What has he done wrong?" Pilate asked. But they shouted all the louder, "Crucify him!"
Pilate saw that he was getting nowhere. Instead, the crowds began to grow angry. So he took water and washed his hands before them. "I am innocent of this man's death," he said. "You are responsible!"
So all the people answered together and said, "We will assume the guilt of innocent blood for the killing of his son!"
Pilate released Barabbas. But he flogged Jesus. Then he handed him over to be crucified on a cross. The soldiers mocked Jesus.
The governor's soldiers took Jesus to the palace, which is called the Praetorian, and all the other soldiers of the governor gathered around him.
They stripped him and dressed him in a purple robe.
Then they braided thorn branches, put them on his head. They gave him a reed in his right hand. They knelt down before him and mocked him. They said, "We honor you, King of the Jews!"
They spat on him. They hit him on the head with a stick again and again.
And after they mocked him, they took the robe off him and put his own clothes on him. Then they led him away to be crucified. Jesus is crucified.
And as they were going out of the city, they met a man of Cyrene, named Simon, they compelled him to bear the cross.
They arrived at a place called Golgotha. And the word Golgotha means the place of the skull.
There they mixed the wine with bitter spices and offered it to Jesus to drink. After tasting it, He refused to drink it.
And when they crucified him, they divided his garments among them, by casting lots.
They sat and watched him there.
Above his head they placed his written accusation: "This is Jesus, King of the Jews."
And with him are two men from the thieves, one of them on his right, and the other on his left.
And those who passed by him scoffed at Jesus and ridiculed him. And they shake their heads.
And he said, "So you are going to destroy the temple and rebuild it in three days? Then save yourself! Come down from the cross, if you are the son of God!"
In the same way, the chief priests, the scribes, and the elders mocked him.
"He saved others," they said. "But he cannot save himself! He is the King of Israel! Let him come down now from the cross! And we will believe in him."
He trusts in God. Let God save him now if he wants to. He is the one who said: "I am the Son of God."
And in the same way, the two thieves who were crucified with Jesus also mocked him. The death of Jesus.
From noon until three o'clock, the entire earth was covered in darkness.
About the third hour, Jesus cried out with a loud voice, saying, "Eli, Eli, lama sabachthani?" which means, "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?" (Psalm 22:1)
Some of those standing there heard Jesus cry out. They said, "He is calling Elijah."
And immediately one of them ran and brought a sponge and filled it with sour wine and put it on a reed and offered it to Jesus' mouth to drink.
And the others said, "Let him alone, Lord; for let us see whether Elias will come to deliver him."
After Jesus cried out again in a loud voice, he died.
At that moment, the veil of the temple was torn in two, from top to bottom. The earth shook. Rocks split.
The tombs were opened. The bodies of many holy people who had died were raised to life.
And they came out of the tombs. And after Jesus was raised to life, they went to the holy city. And there they appeared to many people.
When the Roman centurion and those who were guarding Jesus saw the earthquake and all that had happened, they were terrified and exclaimed, "Truly, this was the Son of God!"
And not far away, many women were watching. They had followed Jesus from Galilee to serve him.
Mary Magdalene was one of them. And Mary the mother of James and Josi, was there also. And the mother of the sons of Zebedee. Christ was buried.
And when the night came, a rich man who was from the city of Jericho, named Joseph, who was a follower of Jesus, drew near.
He went to Pilate and asked for the body of Jesus. Pilate commanded that he should be given.
Joseph took the body and wrapped it in a clean linen cloth.
He placed him in his new private tomb which he had carved out of the rock. Then he rolled a large stone in front of the entrance to the tomb. Then he went away.
Mary Magdalene and the other Mary sat there opposite the tomb. The guards are at the tomb.
And on the following day, the one after the preparation day, the chief priests and the Pharisees went to Pilate.
"Sir," they said, "we recall something that deceiver said while he was still alive. He claimed, 'After three days I will rise again.'"
So, give the order to make the tomb secure until the third day. For if you do not, then his disciples may come and steal away the body. And then they will go and tell the people that Jesus has come back from the dead. And this last lie will be even worse than the first one."
"Take some guards with you," Pilate answered. "Go. Make the tomb as secure as possible."
So they went and secured the tomb. They placed a seal on the stone and appointed guards.
The resurrection of Jesus from the dead.
Now Saturday has already passed. It is the dawn of the first day of the week. Mary Magdalene and the other Mary went to look at the tomb.
A strong earthquake occurred. An angel of the Lord descended from heaven. The angel went to the tomb, moved the stone, and sat on it.
His body shone like lightning. His clothes were white as snow.
The guards were so afraid of him that they trembled and became like the dead.
The angel said to the two women, "Do not be afraid. I know that you are looking for Jesus of Nazareth, who was crucified."
He is not here! He has risen, just as he said he would! Come and see the place where he lay.
Go quickly! Tell his disciples, "He has been raised from the dead. He is going before you to Galilee. There you will see him." Now I have told you."
And they came out from the tomb with trembling and great joy, and ran to tell His disciples.
And behold, Jesus met them, saying, "Peace!" And they came near and held His feet and worshiped Him.
Then Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid. Go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee. There they will see me." The guards' report to the chief priests.
And as the women were on their way, certain guards entered the city; and they reported to the chief priests all that had happened.
And when the priests and the scribes had gathered with the elders, they took counsel together. And they gave much silver to the soldiers.
So they told the soldiers, "Tell them, 'His disciples came at night and stole him while we were sleeping.' "
If the governor hears about this report, we will pay him a bribe. This will keep you out of trouble."
The soldiers took the money and did as they were commanded. This story spread among the Jews to this day. Jesus' final commandments to his disciples.
Then the eleven disciples went to Galilee. They went to the mountain where Jesus had told them to go.
When they saw it, they worshipped it, but some of them continued to have doubts.
Then Jesus came to them and said, "All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me.
And baptize them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.
Teach them to obey all that I have commanded you. And remember that I am with you always, to the end."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
This is the beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
In ancient times, the prophet Isaiah wrote: "Behold, I will send my messenger, and he will prepare the way before me. A voice cries out in the wilderness: 'Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight.'" (Malachi 3:1)
A messenger calls in the wilderness: Prepare the way of the Lord, make straight paths for him. Every valley shall be lifted up, and every mountain and hill shall be made low; and the uneven ground shall become level, and the rough places shall be made smooth.
Yahya came, and people went out into the wilderness. He was preaching, saying, "There is one who comes after me who is mightier than I. I am not worthy to untie the thong of his sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire. His winnowing fan is in his hand, and he will thoroughly clear his threshing floor and gather his wheat into the barn. But he will burn up the chaff with unquenchable fire." Then many women came to him with one accord, saying, "Blessed are the womb and the breasts which have nourished you." But he said to them, "Blessed rather are those who hear the word of God and keep it." And when Jesus finished these parables, he departed from there.
All the people of the countryside from the region of Judea, and all the people of Jerusalem, went out to him. When they acknowledged their sins, John baptized them in the river Jordan.
John wore clothes made of camel hair. He wore a leather belt around his waist. And he ate locusts and wild honey.
Here is what John was proclaiming: "After me, someone will come who is more powerful than I am. I am not worthy to stoop down and untie the thong of his sandals."
I baptize you with water. But he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." Jesus baptizes and is tempted
In those days Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee and was baptized by John in the Jordan.
And John bore witness, saying: "I saw the Spirit descend from heaven like a dove, and it remained on him."
A voice from heaven spoke to him, saying, "You are my beloved Son; in you I am very well pleased."
Immediately the Spirit drove Jesus into the wilderness.
Jesus was in the wilderness for forty days. There, Satan tested him. The wild beasts did not harm Jesus. Angels took care of him. Jesus chooses the first disciples.
After John was put in prison, Jesus went into Galilee, proclaiming the good news of God.
He said, "The time has come. The kingdom of God is near. Turn away from your sins and believe the good news!"
One day Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There he saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
Jesus said, "Come, follow Me." "I will make you fishers of men."
Immediately they left their nets and followed him.
Then Jesus walked a little further. As he was walking, he saw James and his brother John, the sons of Zebedee. They were in a boat mending their nets.
And immediately he called them. So they left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men, and went after Jesus. Jesus Expels a Demon.
Jesus and those who were with him went to Capernaum. And when the Sabbath came, he entered the synagogue. And there he began to teach.
And the audience was amazed at his teaching. He taught them as one having authority. He did not teach them like the scribes.
At that moment, a man in their synagogue cried out. He was being controlled by an evil spirit. And he said,
What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are, you are the Holy One of God!
Jesus said firmly, "Be quiet!" Then He said, "Come out of him!"
The man violently shook from the evil spirit. Then it left him with a scream.
And everyone marveled at the power of the word, and they said, "What is this? Is it a new teaching with all this authority? Even the evil spirit commands it, and it obeys him."
News of Jesus spread quickly throughout Galilee. Jesus heals many people.
Jesus and those who were with him left the synagogue and went directly with James and John to the house of Simon and Andrew.
Simon's mother-in-law was lying in bed, suffering from a fever. They told Jesus about her.
He went to her, took her hand, helped her up, and the fever left her. Then she began to serve them.
That evening, after sunset, the people brought to Jesus all the sick. And they also brought all who were possessed by demons.
All the townspeople gathered at the gate.
Jesus healed many of them. They were all people with diseases. Jesus also drove out many demons. But he did not let the demons speak, because they knew who he was. Jesus prays in a quiet place.
It was very early in the morning, and still dark. Jesus got up and left the house. He went to a place where he could be alone. And there he prayed.
Simon and his friends went to look for Jesus.
When they found him, they cried out, "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus said, "Let's go to another place. I want to go to the nearby towns. I must preach there too. That is why I came."
He went throughout Galilee, teaching in their synagogues and preaching the good news of the kingdom. And he cast out demons. Jesus Heals a Leper
A man who had a skin disease came to Jesus. He knelt before Jesus and begged him, saying, "If you are willing, you can make me clean."
Jesus was filled with deep concern. He reached out and touched the man. "I am willing to do this," he said. "Be 'clean'!"
No sooner had he left than the illness departed from him, and he became healthy.
Jesus immediately dismissed him, warning him severely.
"Don't tell anyone about this," he said. "Go and present yourself to the priest. Make the sacrifices that Moses commanded. You will be a witness to the priest and the people that you are 'clean.'"
But that man went out and began to speak at once. And he spread the news to everyone. So Jesus could no longer enter any city openly. But he remained outside in desolate places. Yet people would come to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who cannot walk.
After a few days, Jesus entered Capernaum again. The people heard that he had returned home.
A large crowd of people gathered until there was no more room. There was not even room outside the door. And Jesus taught them the word.
Four of those who came were carrying a man who could not walk.
But they could not bring him to Jesus because of the crowd. So they made an opening in the roof above Jesus. Then they lowered the man on his bed through that opening.
Jesus saw their faith. He said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
And there were some teachers of the law sitting there, and they were questioning in their hearts,
Why does this man speak in this manner? He is saying something extremely wicked! Only God can forgive sins!
Jesus perceived their thoughts, and said to them, "Why do you reason about these things?"
Which is easier, to tell this man, "Your sins are forgiven"? or to say, "Rise, take up your bed and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then Jesus spoke to the man who could not walk.
"I tell you," he said, "get up. Take your carpet and go to your house."
The man stood up and took his mat. Then he walked away, while everyone was watching. All the people were amazed. And they praised God and said, "We have never seen anything like this!" Jesus Chooses Matthew
Jesus went out again beside the Sea of Galilee. A great multitude came to him. So he taught them.
As Jesus was walking down the road, he saw Levi son of Alphaeus sitting in the tax collector's booth. He said to him, "Follow me." And Levi got up and followed Jesus.
Later, Jesus was having dinner at the house of Levi. Many tax collectors and "sinners" were eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large crowd that followed Jesus.
And there were Pharisees who were legal teachers, and they saw Jesus eating with "sinners" and tax collectors. And they came and asked his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and 'sinners'?"
Jesus heard this. And he said to them, "Those who are healthy do not need a physician, but those who are sick. I did not come to call righteous persons to repentance, but I came to call sinners to repentance."
And John's disciples and the Pharisees were fasting. And people came to Jesus. And they said to him, "John's disciples fast, and the disciples of the Pharisees. But your disciples are not fasting. Why?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them? They cannot fast as long as the bridegroom is with them.
But the time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them. On that day they will go away without eating.
People don't patch a piece of new cloth onto worn clothes. If they did that, the new piece would tear from the old piece. That would make the rip even worse.
People don't put new wine into old wine skins. Otherwise, the wine will burst the skins. Both the wine and the skins will be ruined. Instead, everyone puts new wine into new skins." Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
One Saturday, Jesus was walking with his disciples through wheat fields. The disciples began to break off some heads of wheat.
So the Pharisees said to him, "Look! Why is he doing this on the Sabbath? It is not lawful to do any work on the Sabbath." And the Pharisees came and asked him, "Why is it that you do what is not lawful to do on the Sabbath?"
So he answered, "Have you never read about what David did? He and his men were hungry. They needed food.
This was in the days of Abiathar, the high priest. David went into the house of God and ate of the holy bread. Only priests were allowed to eat it. David also gave some of it to his commanders.
Then Jesus said to them, "The Son of Man is also Lord of the Sabbath."
So the Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath."
Healing on the Sabbath.
Jesus entered the synagogue at another time. There was a man whose hand was withered and crooked.
Some of the Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. They were watching him closely, wanting to see if he would heal the man on the Sabbath.
Jesus spoke to the man with the weak and twisted hand. "Stand in front of everyone," he said.
Then Jesus asked them, "What does the Law say we must do on the Sabbath? Should we do good? Or should we do evil? Should we save life? Or should we kill?" But no one answered him.
Jesus looked at them angrily. He was very disturbed because their hearts were hardened. Then He said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." So he stretched it out, and his hand was restored, whole and sound.
Then the Pharisees went out and took counsel with the Herodians against Jesus, how they might destroy him. And many multitudes followed him.
Jesus went to the Sea of Galilee with his disciples. A great multitude from Galilee followed him.
The people heard about everything Jesus was doing. Many people came to him from Judea, Jerusalem, and from the region east of the Jordan River, from Tyre and Sidon.
Because of the crowd, Jesus told his disciples to bring a small boat for him to use. This would prevent people from crowding around him.
Jesus healed many people. Therefore, the sick were pushing to touch him.
And when the people who had unclean spirits saw that he spoke to them, a great multitude fell to the ground before him. And the unclean spirits cried out and said, "You are the Son of God!"
But Jesus commanded them not to tell anyone about him. Jesus appoints the twelve apostles.
Jesus went up the side of a mountain. He called certain people to come to him, and they did.
He appointed twelve of them, whom he named apostles. They would be with him from then on, and he would also send them out to preach.
They will have the authority to cast out demons.
Therefore, Jesus appointed the twelve. Simon was one of them. Jesus gave him the name Peter.
And there was James the son of Zebedee and his brother John. And Jesus surnamed them Boanerges. And Boanerges means Sons of Thunder.
And James and John, the sons of Zebedee. And Titus and Simon the Zealot.
Judas Iscariot was one of them too. He was the one who would later betray Jesus into the hands of his enemies. Jesus and Beelzebub
Jesus entered a house. And once again a large crowd gathered. It was so large that Jesus and his disciples couldn't even eat.
His family heard about this. So they went to take care of it. They said, "He's out of his mind."
And there were some teachers of the law, who came down from Jerusalem; and they said, "This man is possessed by a demon! He is driving out demons by the power of the prince of demons."
Jesus called them and spoke to them in parables, saying, "How can a demon cast out a demon?"
If an angel fights against his kingdom, he will not endure.
If a family divides, it cannot endure.
